1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,324
Kieran:

2
00:00:00,324 --> 00:00:03,680
So Jane – you’ll be off to Denmark soon to do your work placement.

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:04,163
Jane:

4
00:00:04,163 --> 00:00:09,813
Yes, I’m really looking forward to it and I’ve just started packing up all

5
00:00:09,813 --> 00:00:11,120
my books to put in storage.

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:11,323
Kieran:

7
00:00:11,323 --> 00:00:13,510
Well, I hope they don’t get spoilt.

8
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:13,671
Jane:

9
00:00:13,671 --> 00:00:18,276
It’s OK – my grandfather works in a bookshop and he told me how

10
00:00:18,276 --> 00:00:19,080
to pack them.

11
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:19,386
Kieran:

12
00:00:19,386 --> 00:00:19,650
Oh,

13
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:20,730
that’s helpful.

14
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:20,952
Jane:

15
00:00:20,952 --> 00:00:25,404
He says you have to support the spine otherwise the paper can come away

16
00:00:25,404 --> 00:00:26,270
from the cover.

17
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:26,616
Kieran:

18
00:00:26,616 --> 00:00:28,080
Yeah – that’s obvious.

19
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:28,280
Jane:

20
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:33,010
He also told me to pack them flat in the box not on their side – again

21
00:00:33,010 --> 00:00:38,061
because they can bend and if you leave them like that for, say, a

22
00:00:38,061 --> 00:00:42,330
year, it’s quite hard to get them back to their normal shape.

23
00:00:42,330 --> 00:00:42,531
Kieran:

24
00:00:42,531 --> 00:00:45,104
Well, it’s pretty clear that ruins them,

25
00:00:45,104 --> 00:00:48,320
but a lot of people just can’t be bothered to protect their books.

26
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:48,561
Jane:

27
00:00:48,561 --> 00:00:53,167
He always says it’s such a shame that publishers don’t use better-quality

28
00:00:53,167 --> 00:00:53,490
paper.

29
00:00:53,490 --> 00:00:53,672
Kieran:

30
00:00:53,672 --> 00:00:56,840
It’s the acid in the paper that causes the problem, isn’t it?

31
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:57,242
Jane:

32
00:00:57,242 --> 00:00:59,594
Yeah – that’s why old books go yellow.

33
00:00:59,594 --> 00:01:04,602
You know some of the books my grandfather’s given me are like that

34
00:01:04,602 --> 00:01:04,940
already.

35
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:04,990
Kieran:

36
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:05,710
Oh .

37
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:05,760
.

38
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:05,810
.

39
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:06,029
Jane:

40
00:01:06,029 --> 00:01:09,862
I should dump them really if they’re going to deteriorate further,

41
00:01:09,862 --> 00:01:11,459
but I’d feel bad.

42
00:01:11,459 --> 00:01:13,694
They’ll always remind me of him.

43
00:01:13,694 --> 00:01:15,930
He’s quite a collector, you know.

44
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:16,143
Kieran:

45
00:01:16,143 --> 00:01:17,630
Well, if they’re important to you .

46
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:17,680
.

47
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:17,730
.

48
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:18,002
Jane:

49
00:01:18,002 --> 00:01:20,980
Yeah – I’d regret just throwing them away.

50
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:21,289
Kieran:

51
00:01:21,289 --> 00:01:25,002
You know, maybe it’s because I was taught to treasure books .

52
00:01:25,002 --> 00:01:25,052
.

53
00:01:25,052 --> 00:01:25,102
.

54
00:01:25,102 --> 00:01:28,406
but I hate seeing students force open the pages – of paperbacks.

55
00:01:28,406 --> 00:01:31,500
They press so hard they end up breaking the spine.

56
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:31,918
Jane:

57
00:01:31,918 --> 00:01:32,755
I know,

58
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:37,357
but unfortunately, paperbacks aren’t designed to last a long time

59
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:39,030
and people know that.

60
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:41,540
Hardbacks aren’t quite as weak.

61
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:41,855
Kieran:

62
00:01:41,855 --> 00:01:43,745
Yeah, they’re different, I suppose.

63
00:01:43,745 --> 00:01:47,840
But I still don’t think people value hardbacks like they used to.

64
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:48,223
Jane:

65
00:01:48,223 --> 00:01:52,053
Well, they aren’t decorative, are they, like other objects.

66
00:01:52,053 --> 00:01:57,797
Plus, nowadays, people don’t keep them out on shelves as much as they used

67
00:01:57,797 --> 00:01:58,180
to.

68
00:01:58,180 --> 00:01:58,456
Kieran:

69
00:01:58,456 --> 00:01:59,837
That’s such a pity.

70
00:01:59,837 --> 00:02:03,428
When I visit someone – if they have, say, a colourful book on a

71
00:02:03,428 --> 00:02:06,190
table, it’s the first thing I’m drawn to.

72
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:06,647
Jane:

73
00:02:06,647 --> 00:02:12,583
I agree – and book covers can be a work of art in themselves.

74
00:02:12,583 --> 00:02:14,410
Some are really eye-catching.

75
00:02:14,410 --> 00:02:14,692
Kieran:

76
00:02:14,692 --> 00:02:17,229
I’ve always been taught to handle books carefully.

77
00:02:17,229 --> 00:02:21,176
If you watch someone take a book off a shelf, well, they usually do

78
00:02:21,176 --> 00:02:21,740
it wrong.

79
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:22,085
Jane:

80
00:02:22,085 --> 00:02:26,919
Ah, my grandfather says, you should put your hand right over the top of

81
00:02:26,919 --> 00:02:27,609
the book .

82
00:02:27,609 --> 00:02:27,659
.

83
00:02:27,659 --> 00:02:27,709
.

84
00:02:27,709 --> 00:02:32,788
or if you can’t do that, pull the other books on the shelf aside

85
00:02:32,788 --> 00:02:35,550
so that you can hold the whole cover.

86
00:02:35,550 --> 00:02:35,787
Kieran:

87
00:02:35,787 --> 00:02:37,210
When did you learn all this?

88
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:37,512
Jane:

89
00:02:37,512 --> 00:02:41,735
He watched me pull a heavy book off the shelf when I was small,

90
00:02:41,735 --> 00:02:44,450
and it fell on the floor and broke apart.

91
00:02:44,450 --> 00:02:44,637
Kieran:

92
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:45,010
Oh dear!

93
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:45,460
Jane:

94
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:47,710
I can still remember it!

95
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:47,960
Kieran:

96
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:49,460
You know what I really like?

97
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:49,745
Jane:

98
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:50,030
What?

99
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:50,348
Kieran:

100
00:02:50,348 --> 00:02:51,940
The smell of new books.

101
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:52,327
Jane:

102
00:02:52,327 --> 00:02:53,100
Me too.

103
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:53,331
Kieran:

104
00:02:53,331 --> 00:02:56,099
My parents used to laugh at me when I was a kid

105
00:02:56,099 --> 00:02:58,176
because I loved putting books up to my nose.

106
00:02:58,176 --> 00:02:59,560
Almost as much as reading them!

107
00:02:59,560 --> 00:02:59,871
Jane:

108
00:02:59,871 --> 00:03:02,360
New books aren’t cheap, though, are they?

109
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:02,560
Kieran:

110
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,360
I guess we’re lucky we can buy them.

111
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:04,718
Jane:

112
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:08,297
My grandfather stocks second-hand books as well as new ones

113
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:11,160
and they don’t smell quite as good.

114
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:11,463
Kieran:

115
00:03:11,463 --> 00:03:14,497
I’d love to have a bookshop like your grandfather.

116
00:03:14,497 --> 00:03:15,710
What’s it like?

117
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:16,101
Jane:

118
00:03:16,101 --> 00:03:21,189
Well, it’s quite big – it’s got two floors and an attic,

119
00:03:21,189 --> 00:03:24,320
and he stocks all kinds of books really.

120
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:24,616
Kieran:

121
00:03:24,616 --> 00:03:28,170
I guess he treasures things like first editions and other rare books.

122
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:28,494
Jane:

123
00:03:28,494 --> 00:03:32,700
Yeah – you might think he’d keep those in the attic or somewhere.

124
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:32,942
Kieran:

125
00:03:32,942 --> 00:03:32,992
.

126
00:03:32,992 --> 00:03:33,042
.

127
00:03:33,042 --> 00:03:33,092
.

128
00:03:33,092 --> 00:03:34,150
so they’d be hidden?

129
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:34,462
Jane:

130
00:03:34,462 --> 00:03:34,774
Yeah.

131
00:03:34,774 --> 00:03:37,894
But he likes people to know that he has them.

132
00:03:37,894 --> 00:03:42,262
So, he puts them out in the shop but makes sure you need a

133
00:03:42,262 --> 00:03:43,510
ladder to get them.

134
00:03:43,510 --> 00:03:43,799
Kieran:

135
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:44,087
Right.

136
00:03:44,087 --> 00:03:45,530
That would prevent any thefts!

137
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:46,055
Jane:

138
00:03:46,055 --> 00:03:46,580
Uhuh.

139
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:46,833
Kieran:

140
00:03:46,833 --> 00:03:48,350
Does he stock books for children?

141
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:48,698
Jane:

142
00:03:48,698 --> 00:03:49,394
He does.

143
00:03:49,394 --> 00:03:52,177
He particularly likes to encourage kids to read;

144
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:58,788
he always says that he used to sit under the stairs as a child with a pile of books

145
00:03:58,788 --> 00:04:00,180
and read them all.

146
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:00,354
Kieran:

147
00:04:00,354 --> 00:04:01,570
Is that where he keeps them, then?

148
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:01,949
Jane:

149
00:04:01,949 --> 00:04:07,251
Not exactly – he’s got a dedicated area on the ground floor with cushions

150
00:04:07,251 --> 00:04:11,039
so that parents can enter with their toddlers, go there

151
00:04:11,039 --> 00:04:13,690
and spend some time reading to them.

152
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:14,010
Kieran:

153
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:14,650
Oh cool.

154
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:14,974
Jane:

155
00:04:14,974 --> 00:04:18,862
And then there’s a place for pushchairs by the front door.

156
00:04:18,862 --> 00:04:21,130
And a café if anyone needs refreshments.

157
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:21,305
Kieran:

158
00:04:21,305 --> 00:04:22,180
That’s good to know.

159
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:22,462
Jane:

160
00:04:22,462 --> 00:04:26,982
As I said, it’s a big shop and there’s a storage area out the

161
00:04:26,982 --> 00:04:27,830
back as well.

162
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:28,117
Kieran:

163
00:04:28,117 --> 00:04:29,838
Oh, what does he keep there?

164
00:04:29,838 --> 00:04:31,560
Books he wants to throw away?

165
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:31,936
Jane:

166
00:04:31,936 --> 00:04:36,831
He hardly ever throws anything away – he just leaves unwanted books by the

167
00:04:36,831 --> 00:04:39,090
front door for customers to take.

168
00:04:39,090 --> 00:04:39,382
Kieran:

169
00:04:39,382 --> 00:04:39,673
Well,

170
00:04:39,673 --> 00:04:40,840
that’s very nice.

171
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:41,286
Jane:

172
00:04:41,286 --> 00:04:47,079
Yeah – and books people or institutions have requested, they all go at the

173
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:47,970
far end.

174
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:48,380
Kieran:

175
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:48,790
Oh.

176
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:49,055
Jane:

177
00:04:49,055 --> 00:04:53,820
He thinks it’s best to keep these out of the main shopping area as they’re boxed

178
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:54,350
and new.

179
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:54,612
Kieran:

180
00:04:54,612 --> 00:04:56,450
Did you get your coursebooks from him?

181
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:56,796
Jane:

182
00:04:56,796 --> 00:04:57,141
Naturally.

183
00:04:57,141 --> 00:05:00,251
He stocks books for a lot of the colleges.

184
00:05:00,251 --> 00:05:03,707
He used to keep these books on the first floor,

185
00:05:03,707 --> 00:05:09,236
but now there’s a new university in my hometown, he’s moved them downstairs to

186
00:05:09,236 --> 00:05:10,272
attract the students.

187
00:05:10,272 --> 00:05:15,110
They’re actually part of the coffee shop, on low shelves all around it.

188
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:15,365
Kieran:

189
00:05:15,365 --> 00:05:16,128
Pretty central then.

190
00:05:16,128 --> 00:05:18,420
You’ll have to take me there some time!
